Setup is a place you can leave
=============================

First run was a room with no door: once you were in it, the only way out was to finish it. Now the way
out is on every screen, and it goes back to the beginning rather than out to the app, because "back"
means back.

  * the intro keeps one exit — **Skip setup, straight to the app** — and it is labelled as skipping;
  * every step after it carries **← Back to the start**, which returns to the first onboarding screen;
  * the main page carries **Just give me the table**, and so does the intro: provider, key, model,
    browser, memory folder, encryption, one table, four fields of it required;
  * the table itself is a real page (`setup`), not a wizard state, so it can be reached from anywhere
    and its back arrow returns to onboarding.

One bug worth naming: the table was painted by a function that lived inside the wizard's closure, so
the standalone page could not see it and rendered an error instead — "Can't find variable: quickstep".
It is at module scope now, and takes its way-back as an argument rather than reaching for wizard state
it cannot see.

Also in this patch, under the Nora description: **"But I'm not disabled. Why would I want this?"** —
that whether you are disabled, and how, is your own business; that under the ADA and HIPAA you are not
obliged to hand it over to a stranger as the price of using software; that where a corporation's
assistant is a chatbot answering to the corporation, this was built the other way round, beginning as
an assistance device for one person with a disability; that the difference is a category in law rather
than a phrase in a brochure — Title II binds public entities (a courthouse, a clerk's window, a school
office), Title III binds places of public accommodation (a doctor's office, a pharmacy, a lawyer's
waiting room), a federal court under Article III answers to the same rule through Section 504 of the
Rehabilitation Act, and 28 CFR 35.160 requires the entity to furnish appropriate auxiliary aids and
services and to give primary consideration to the person who will use them, which makes a person's own
software the accommodation and the refusal the barrier; that it is built to the standard assistive
software is held to, which is what lets it go places an ordinary assistant does not; and that if you
have no use for that you still get a plain, private assistant that runs on your own machine.
